INT. PIGEON RACING CLUB - BACK ROOM - NIGHT

Folding chairs. A coffee urn. Trophies with tiny gilded birds. A banner reads "ANNUAL MEMBERSHIP MEETING."

GLORIA HUANG chairs the meeting from a card table. She taps a gavel.

GLORIA
Item four. The clubhouse roof.

HAROLD OYELARAN, seventies, raises a hand. He wears a bow tie and a tired expression.

HAROLD
I move we ignore it.

MARISOL TREVIÑO, a retired postal worker, shakes her head. She holds a casserole dish on her lap.

MARISOL
Harold, it rains inside.

HAROLD
So do the birds.

DEACON PRATT, the treasurer, adjusts his hearing aid and beams at no one.

DEACON
What did he say?

CORNELIA ASHBY-REID sits beside him, knitting something blue. She leans over and speaks into his ear.

CORNELIA
Harold wants to ignore the roof.

DEACON
Bold.

GLORIA
We have eleven hundred dollars.

MARISOL
The roofer wants four thousand.

GLORIA
We could hold a raffle.

HAROLD
We held one last year.

GLORIA
And?

HAROLD
Somebody won the casserole.

All eyes drop to Marisol's dish. She shrugs.

MARISOL
It was a very good casserole.

TEDDY NKEMELU, the youngest member by forty years, sits in the back with a bag of seed. He pipes up.

TEDDY
What if we race for it?

GLORIA
Race what?

TEDDY
The birds. Winner takes a share. Entry fee goes to the roof.

Gloria looks at Harold. He looks at Marisol. She looks at the casserole.

She nods first. Then he does. Then everyone.

Gloria raises the gavel.

GLORIA (CONT'D)
Motion carried. Somebody write that down.

Cornelia holds up her knitting. It is, on closer look, a tiny sweater for a pigeon.

CORNELIA
Already on it.
